# Check every text file out with LF on every platform.
#
# Without this, Git for Windows defaults to `core.autocrlf=true`, so a Windows
# checkout converts LF to CRLF. Anything that compares generated text against a
# checked-in file then fails on Windows and only on Windows, which is how
# `tests/api_docs.rs` failed the first time Varda's tests ran there: the
# reference table it renders uses `\n`, the file it read back had `\r\n`.
#
# Regenerating, as that test's own panic message suggests, would not have fixed
# it. The regenerated file is LF, and the next checkout converts it back.
#
# Repository content is already LF, having been committed from macOS and Linux,
# so this changes checkout behaviour rather than any stored content.
* text=auto eol=lf

# The exception. This is a Windows CI script, and CRLF is what it gets today
# under `core.autocrlf`; pinning it keeps that exactly as it is rather than
# changing a working build script as a side effect of a docs fix.
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# Never translate these.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.ico binary
*.icns binary
*.wgsl text eol=lf
